<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><title>The man behind the Pirate · Marco Pantani</title><meta name="description" content="A severe crash cost Marco Pantani the entire 1996 season. He returned to win the Giro and Tour, but the man’s story is more complex."/><link rel="canonical" href={canonical}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="da" href="https://teob.dk/historier/marco-pantani-manden-bag-piraten/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href={canonical}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://teob.dk/historier/marco-pantani-manden-bag-piraten/"/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:locale" content="en_GB"/><meta property="og:site_name" content="The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:title" content="The man behind the Pirate"/><meta property="og:description" content="Marco Pantani returned from a shattered leg to win the Giro and Tour. A tribute to the rider and the vulnerable man."/><meta property="og:url" content={canonical}/><meta property="og:image" content={ogImage}/><meta property="og:image:alt" content="Portrait of Marco Pantani in a Mercatone Uno jersey"/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><meta name="twitter:title" content="The man behind the Pirate"/><meta name="twitter:description" content="Marco Pantani’s road from the 1995 crash to the 1998 Giro–Tour double."/><meta name="twitter:image" content={ogImage}/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body>
<header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href={`${base}/en/`}><span class="brand-mark">E<span>B</span></span><span>The Evolution<br/>of Bikes</span></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`} aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/`}>Eras</a><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>Bikes</a><a href={`${base}/en/components/`}>Components</a><a href={`${base}/en/about/`}>About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="language-switch" href={`${base}/historier/marco-pantani-manden-bag-piraten/`} lang="da">DA</a><span>/</span><strong>EN</strong></div></header>
<main><article class="pantani-story"><header class="story-hero"><p class="section-label">Italy · 1995–2004</p><h1>The man behind the Pirate</h1><p>Marco Pantani returned from a shattered left leg to win both the Giro d’Italia and the Tour de France. That did not make him unbreakable. It makes the story more human.</p></header>
<figure class="story-image hero"><OptimizedImage src={portrait} alt="Marco Pantani in a Mercatone Uno jersey, wearing a bandana and sunglasses" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>Marco Pantani in Luxembourg in 1997. Photo: Aldo Bolzan; scan: Les Meloures. <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marco_Pantani.jpg">Wikimedia Commons</a>, CC BY-SA 3.0.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="story-copy"><p class="lead">On 18 October 1995, Marco Pantani lay on the road during Milano–Torino. A car was in the race route at Pino Torinese, and the collision shattered the tibia and fibula of his left leg.</p><p>A year earlier he had finished second in the Giro and third in the Tour. In 1995 he won at Alpe d’Huez and Guzet-Neige, then took bronze at the World Championships. Now the whole 1996 season disappeared.</p><p>The episode is often used as proof of an unbreakable mind. That is too simple. Pantani returned from an injury that would have ended many careers, but he was not invulnerable. The tension between the fearless racer and the vulnerable man is why he is still remembered.</p><h2>The small rider from Cesenatico</h2><p>Born in Cesena in 1970 and raised in Cesenatico, Pantani began racing with the local Fausto Coppi club. His slight build and abrupt rhythm quickly found the terrain where larger riders struggled. The shaved head, bandana and earring gave him the name <em>Il Pirata</em>. His attacks gave the name its force.</p></div>
<figure class="story-image wide"><OptimizedImage src={alpe} alt="Marco Pantani climbing towards Alpe d'Huez during the 1997 Tour de France"/><figcaption>Pantani on the road to Alpe d’Huez in the 1997 Tour. Photo: Hein Ciere. <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marco_Pantani,_1997.jpg">Wikimedia Commons</a>, CC BY 3.0.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="story-copy"><h2>Back — and down again</h2><p>Pantani returned in 1997 with Mercatone Uno. A cat crossed the road on the descent from Chiunzi during the Giro, causing another crash and retirement. At the Tour he answered with victories at Alpe d’Huez and Morzine and third place overall.</p><p>The comeback peaked in 1998. Pantani won the Giro, then attacked on the Galibier during a cold, wet Tour stage and rode into yellow at Les Deux Alpes. In Paris he became the first Italian Tour winner since Felice Gimondi in 1965 — and remains the most recent rider to win the Giro and Tour in one season.</p></div>
<figure class="story-image wide"><OptimizedImage src={giro} alt="Marco Pantani in the pink leader's jersey in a Giro d'Italia peloton"/><figcaption>Pantani in the Giro’s pink jersey. Photo: Brian Townsley; edited by Petar Milošević. <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marco_Pantani_-_Giro_d%27Italia_(16384453148).jpg">Wikimedia Commons</a>, CC BY 2.0.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="story-copy"><h2>A tribute must include the whole man</h2><p>In 1999 Pantani led the Giro when a blood test at Madonna di Campiglio recorded a haematocrit above the permitted threshold. He was removed from the race on health grounds. Later evidence of unusually high and fluctuating blood values means his career cannot be separated from cycling’s doping era.</p><p>Campiglio was also a human breaking point. Pantani returned and won two Tour stages in 2000, but never regained the same consistency. Distrust, court cases, isolation, depression and cocaine abuse increasingly took over. On 14 February 2004 he was found dead in a Rimini hotel, aged 34.</p><p>A tribute need not soften the darkness. Pantani’s greatness was not that he could never break. It was found in the moments when a small rider made an entire mountain wait for his next attack — and in the long road back after 1995.</p></div>
<figure class="story-image wide"><OptimizedImage src={briancon} alt="Marco Pantani cornering during the 2000 Tour de France"/><figcaption>Pantani during the 2000 Tour, his final Tour. Photo: Oded Zur. <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pantani_briancon_2000_2.jpg">Wikimedia Commons</a>, CC BY-SA 3.0.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="archive-gallery"><div class="gallery-heading"><p class="section-label">After the Pirate</p><h2>The places where he is remembered</h2></div><div class="gallery-grid"><figure><OptimizedImage src={carpegna} alt="Marco Pantani memorial on Monte Carpegna"/><figcaption>The Monte Carpegna memorial. Photo: Jedesto. <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pantani_Carpegna.jpg">Wikimedia Commons</a>, CC BY-SA 4.0.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={monument} alt="Bronze statue of Marco Pantani on a racing bicycle in Cesenatico"/><figcaption>The Pantani monument in Cesenatico. Photo: Brianza2008. <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monumento_Pantani_Cesenatico.jpg">Wikimedia Commons</a>, CC0.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image notes</p><div><ul><li><a href="https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/marco-pantani_%28Dizionario-Biografico%29/">Treccani: Marco Pantani</a></li><li><a href="https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/ciclismo_%28Enciclopedia-dello-Sport%29/">Treccani: Cycling</a></li><li><a href="https://www.giroditalia.it/en/palmares/">Giro d’Italia: official roll of honour</a></li></ul><p>Each image is reproduced under the licence stated in its caption. None documents the 1995 crash.</p></div></section><nav class="era-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`}>← All stories</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1990-1999/`}>The 1990–1999 era →</a></nav></article></main><footer><div class="brand footer-brand"><span class="brand-mark">E<span>B</span></span><span>The Evolution<br/>of Bikes</span></div><p>A personal collection of vintage bicycles,<br/>stories, brands and components.</p><p class="copyright">© {new Date().getFullYear()} The Evolution of Bikes</p></footer></body></html>
